 ## Overview
-Part-based MHE-augmented NeRF is a method for learning neural volumetric videos of dynamic humans from sparse view videos in minutes with competitive visual quality [S1]. It addresses the challenge of quickly reconstructing free-viewpoint videos of dynamic humans by decomposing the canonical human body into multiple anatomical parts, assigning each part its own Multiresolution Hash Encoding (MHE)-augmented NeRF network to efficiently distribute representational power [S1]. Unlike a single-resolution representation that treats all body parts as equally complex, this method adapts the hash table size per part based on local geometric and texture complexity, accelerating convergence while maintaining high fidelity [S1].
+Part-based MHE-augmented NeRF is a method for learning neural volumetric videos of dynamic humans from sparse view videos in minutes with competitive visual quality [S1]. It addresses the slow convergence of per-scene optimization methods by decomposing the canonical human body into multiple anatomical parts, assigning each part its own Multiresolution Hash Encoding (MHE)-augmented NeRF network with varying resolutions to efficiently distribute representational power [S1]. Unlike single-resolution voxelized representations or continuous implicit functions that treat the entire body uniformly, this method leverages a 2D motion parameterization scheme on a parametric mesh (SMPL) to accelerate deformation field learning [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method represents a dynamic human as a canonical neural radiance field and a motion field, learned from videos through differentiable rendering [S1]. The core innovation is the part-based voxelized human representation and a 2D motion parameterization scheme [S1].
-
-### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs:** Sparse multi-view video frames of a human performer; SMPL parametric human model parameters (pose $\rho$, shape $\beta$); camera intrinsics and extrinsics [S1].
-*   **Outputs:** Photorealistic novel view renderings of the dynamic human for any frame $t$ and camera pose [S1].
-
-### Data Structures
-
-**1. SMPL Model and Part Decomposition**
-The human body is decomposed into $K$ parts based on the SMPL template mesh $M=(V, E)$, where $V$ represents vertices and $E$ represents edges [S1].
-*   **Blend Weights:** Each vertex $v_i$ has a blend weight vector $w_i$ indicating influence of $J$ bones [S1].
-*   **Part Definition:** For each part $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$, a set of bones $\Omega_k$ is defined [S1]. The mesh for part $k$ is $M_k = (V_k, E_k)$, where:
-    $$ V_k = \{v_i \mid \arg\max w_i \in \Omega_k\} $$
-    $$ E_k = \{(v_i, v_j) \mid v_i \in V_k, v_j \in V_k\} $$
+**Inputs:**
+- A sequence of sparse multi-view videos of a dynamic human.
+- SMPL parameters (pose $\rho$, shape $\beta$) for each frame, or a pre-estimated SMPL mesh sequence [S1].
+- Camera intrinsics and extrinsics for the input views [S1].
+
+**Outputs:**
+- A set of $K$ MHE-augmented NeRF networks, one for each human part $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$, representing the canonical space [S1].
+- A latent embedding $l_t$ for each frame $t$ [S1].
+- Rendered novel view images for any camera pose and time $t$ [S1].
+
+**Data Structures:**
+1.  **SMPL Mesh Model:** A parametric human model $M=(V, E)$ with vertices $V$ and edges $E$. Each vertex $v_i$ has a blend weight $w_i \in \mathbb{R}^{J}$, where $J$ is the number of bones [S1].
+2.  **Part Decomposition:** The body is divided into $K$ parts. For each part $k$, a set of bones $\Omega_k$ is defined. The mesh for part $k$ is $M_k = (V_k, E_k)$, where:
+    $$
+    V_k = \{v_i \mid \arg\max w_i \in \Omega_k\}
+    $$
+    $$
+    E_k = \{(v_i, v_j) \mid v_i \in V_k, v_j \in V_k\}
