 ## Subtopics
 
-### Multiresolution Hash Encoding (MHE)
-A technique to embed input coordinates to a high-dimensional space using trainable hash tables, allowing for efficient and high-resolution representation [S1]. It sets itself apart by enabling spatially varying resolution and faster training compared to dense voxel grids [G]. Choose it when memory efficiency and high-frequency detail are needed [G]. [S1]
-
-### Inverse Linear Blend Skinning (LBS)
-A method to transform points from world space to unposed space using the inverse of the weighted sum of bone transformations [S1]. It is used as the base motion field before residual deformation [S1].
-
-### SMPL Model
-A parametric human model used to define the body shape, pose, and part decomposition [S1]. It provides the mesh, blend weights, and UV coordinates necessary for the part-based representation [S1].
+### MHE (Multiresolution Hash Encoding)
+A technique to encode coordinates into a high-dimensional space using hash tables of trainable features, allowing for efficient and high-resolution representation [S1]. It sets itself apart by enabling spatially varying resolution and fast lookup. Choose it when memory efficiency and high-frequency detail are needed in implicit fields. [48-multiresolution-hash-encoding.md]
+
+### SMPL (Skinned Multi-Person Linear model)
+A parametric human body model used to define the canonical shape and deformation. It provides the mesh, blend weights, and pose parameters. Choose it when working with dynamic human reconstruction. [43-smpl.md]
+
+### Inverse LBS (Linear Blend Skinning)
+A method to transform points from posed space to canonical space by inverting the weighted sum of bone transformations. It sets itself apart by providing a differentiable approximation of inverse kinematics. Choose it for pose-dependent deformation. [35-inverse-lbs.md]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is significantly reduced compared to prior methods [S1]. It is 100 times faster than Neural Body, which typically requires more than 10 hours of training [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory consumption is reduced by adapting the hash table size per part [S1]. Uniformly large tables are avoided to prevent excessive memory usage [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the ZJU-MoCap dataset, the method achieves competitive PSNR [S1]. Ablation studies show that the part-based approach (286M params) converges faster and yields higher PSNR than a single MHE-augmented NeRF for the whole body (302M params) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast training (minutes vs. hours), competitive visual quality, efficient distribution of representational power [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a parametric human model (SMPL) for part decomposition and motion parameterization [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When rapid reconstruction of dynamic humans from sparse views is required, and a parametric model is available [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity reconstruction of non-human subjects or subjects without a good parametric model is needed [G].
